# Copy to .env for npm start or Docker Compose; keep the real file private.
# Required: Uptime Goblin always requires an admin password and refuses to start (exit status 78)
# while this is empty or missing and there is no saved auth.json. 12 to 1024 characters.
# Sign in as admin. Keep it inside the single quotes: Docker Compose would otherwise treat
# $ sequences in it as variables, and npm start would end an unquoted value at a # (the
# password would then be shorter there than under Compose). Generate a unique password
# locally; do not reuse a service credential. This file holds it: keep it private
# (chmod 600 .env); npm start warns while others can read it.
GOBLIN_PASSWORD=''
# Exact browser Host values, comma-separated; include non-default ports.
GOBLIN_ALLOWED_HOSTS=localhost:4317
# Docker Compose publishes only on loopback unless this is changed. On another address Uptime Goblin
# refuses to start until GOBLIN_TRUST_PROXY names your HTTPS proxy or the
# GOBLIN_INSECURE_HTTP_SESSIONS line below is set (1, or 0); with 1 it warns at every start.
GOBLIN_BIND_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1
# Docker Compose's limits for the container (1 GiB of memory, 1 CPU, 256 processes) are in
# compose.yaml, not here: change them in compose.override.yaml (docs/installation.md).
# Under Docker Compose and the systemd unit Uptime Goblin refuses to start (exit status 78) unless each
# of them is in force in its own cgroup. Only on a platform that cannot apply one (a NAS kernel
# without CPU bandwidth control where Docker also discards a CPU set, an LXC whose host does
# not delegate a controller), name it here: memory, cpu and pids, comma-separated, no spaces.
# Uptime Goblin then starts without it and says so at every start (docs/installation.md, Resource
# limits). npm start does not check its limits, but still refuses a malformed value. The image
# and the unit start Uptime Goblin with --supervised, which no setting here turns off: an
# GOBLIN_SUPERVISED=0 is ignored, and a value that is neither yes nor no refuses the start.
# GOBLIN_ALLOW_UNLIMITED=cpu
# Your reverse proxy, so failed sign-ins are throttled per real client (last
# X-Forwarded-For entry) instead of per proxy: loopback for a proxy on this host,
# or the proxy's address/CIDR (with Compose, the address the proxy connects from,
# e.g. the Docker network gateway). Blank when clients connect to Uptime Goblin directly.
GOBLIN_TRUST_PROXY=
# How many connections one client (one address; an IPv6 /64) may hold open at once, 8 to
# 256 (32 when empty); the proxy above is not limited by it. Everyone behind one address
# shares it: under Docker Compose every browser on the published port when Docker hands
# their connections over from its gateway, the clients of a proxy not named above, and
# everything on this computer that connects from 127.0.0.1. Name the proxy above, or raise
# this if pages fail to load there; Uptime Goblin logs the connections it closes this way, at most
# one line a minute. A change needs a restart.
# GOBLIN_MAX_CONNECTIONS_PER_CLIENT=32
# Set to 1 to allow sources on loopback (127.0.0.1, ::1, localhost), for example an
# exporter on this same host when running natively. Refused by default. Under Docker,
# loopback is the container itself; reach host services by their LAN address instead.
GOBLIN_ALLOW_LOOPBACK=
# Sign-in over plain HTTP: the password at each sign-in and the session cookie (without
# Secure) cross the network unencrypted, with a banner on every page. Browsers sign in
# over HTTPS through a reverse proxy, or from this computer (http://localhost:4317 with
# npm start). Browsing the Compose port directly (http://localhost:4317) is plain HTTP
# too, because inside the container the peer is the Docker gateway. While this line
# stays commented out, Compose passes compose-default: on while GOBLIN_BIND_ADDRESS is a
# loopback address (127.0.0.1, only the Docker host reaches the port), off on another
# address when GOBLIN_TRUST_PROXY names your HTTPS proxy, and otherwise Uptime Goblin refuses to
# start (exit status 78) until you choose: 1 keeps plain-HTTP sign-in on that network,
# 0 refuses it (an empty value is off too; deleting the line is not, under Compose).
# Set 0 once Uptime Goblin is served through an HTTPS proxy. A change needs a restart (docker
# compose up -d, or restart npm start). Outside Compose, compose-default is no yes or no:
# a start warning, and off. It also lets scripts sign in with HTTP Basic over plain HTTP
# (refused otherwise). With npm start, Basic through a proxy on this computer that sends
# no forwarding headers and keeps a loopback Host needs no such line: Uptime Goblin takes that
# proxy for this computer, accepts Basic through it and logs no warning. Make such a
# proxy send X-Forwarded-For and X-Forwarded-Proto and set GOBLIN_TRUST_PROXY=loopback.
# (Under Compose a proxy on the Docker host reaches Uptime Goblin from the Docker gateway, so
# it is plain HTTP like any other client.)
# GOBLIN_INSECURE_HTTP_SESSIONS=1
# HTTP Basic sign-in for scripts (deprecated). Browsers sign in on Uptime Goblin's page, but
# they send no Sec-Fetch-Site to a plain-HTTP address on the network (they do to
# localhost, 127.x and [::1]), so wherever Uptime Goblin accepts Basic over plain HTTP a
# browser's remembered 1.4.x Basic login sent to such an address is checked like a
# script's (docs/authentication.md, Scripts and legacy Basic). On for installs upgraded from
# 1.4.x, off for new ones; 1 or 0 decides, and any other value refuses the start. A
# change needs a restart (docker compose up -d).
# GOBLIN_LEGACY_BASIC=1
# Wall displays signed in with a display code (goblin-admin display code; see
# docs/authentication.md, Wall displays): at most this many days (1 to 30, default 30),
# and at most this many days without a request (1 to 7, default 7). Any other value
# refuses the start.
# GOBLIN_DISPLAY_SESSION_DAYS=30
# GOBLIN_DISPLAY_IDLE_DAYS=7
# The audit log (Configure → Audit log). Uptime Goblin prints a checkpoint line to its own log each
# time it records something, whatever these say. GOBLIN_AUDIT_LOG copies every entry there too,
# with who, from which address and browser, and the session id:
# off (the default), stdout, or stdout-full (also what changed; never a secret).
# Keep that log as private as the data.
# GOBLIN_AUDIT_RETENTION_DAYS keeps entries that many days, 30 to 3650 (90 when empty), at
# most 250,000 entries. Docker Compose passes both. A change needs a restart.
GOBLIN_AUDIT_LOG=
GOBLIN_AUDIT_RETENTION_DAYS=
# Optional JSON webhook for alerts; restart Uptime Goblin after editing. Requests are signed
# (X-Goblin-Signature, Ed25519): receivers verify them with the public key under
# Configure → Notifications → Show public key.
# Link-local, multicast and reserved addresses, and a user name or password in the URL, are
# refused. Under Docker Compose 127.0.0.1 is the container itself: use the receiver's LAN
# address. Treat a URL that carries a token like a password.
GOBLIN_WEBHOOK_URL=
# Encryption key for the secrets Uptime Goblin seals (for example the webhook's private signing key).
# For npm start, keep it in a file outside the data directory. If Uptime Goblin has already started
# on the data directory, move its data/secret.key out (a new key would not open the data;
# with GOBLIN_DATA_DIR set, use that directory's secret.key), never over a file you have:
#   test -e ~/.orbit-secret.key && echo '.orbit-secret.key exists: not replaced' || install -m 600 data/secret.key ~/.orbit-secret.key
# then, once Uptime Goblin runs with the line below, delete the copy in the data directory with
#   node --env-file-if-exists=.env lib/cli/goblin-admin.mjs key remove-data-copy
# Otherwise make a new key, private from its first byte, never over a file you have:
#   test -e ~/.orbit-secret.key && echo '.orbit-secret.key exists: keep it' || (umask 077 && openssl rand -hex 32 > ~/.orbit-secret.key)
# Without one, Uptime Goblin generates secret.key inside the data directory (with a warning at each
# start: every copy of the data directory then carries the key). Back the key up separately;
# a backup of the data cannot be started without it. Docker Compose: use compose.secret.yaml,
# named here so that every docker compose command (updates, rollbacks, settings changes,
# goblin-admin) mounts the key; without it a recreated container cannot start (exit 78):
# COMPOSE_FILE=compose.yaml:compose.secret.yaml
# With a compose.override.yaml (it holds cpus: 0 and cpuset: '0' where Docker refuses any limit
# on CPU time), end that line with :compose.override.yaml: Compose reads the file by itself only
# while COMPOSE_FILE is unset, and only for a command given no -f (docs/installation.md,
# Encryption key).
# The two key lines below are for npm start only: Docker Compose ignores them (compose.yaml
# passes neither into the container, and Uptime Goblin would generate /var/lib/uptime-goblin/secret.key instead);
# use compose.secret.yaml there. Give the full path: .env does not expand ~ (Linux:
# /home/you/.orbit-secret.key).
# GOBLIN_SECRET_KEY_FILE=/Users/you/.orbit-secret.key
# GOBLIN_SECRET_KEY works too (64 hex digits), but stays visible in /proc/<pid>/environ;
# prefer GOBLIN_SECRET_KEY_FILE.
# GOBLIN_SECRET_KEY=
# Native npm start only; Compose sets HOST=0.0.0.0 inside its container.
HOST=127.0.0.1
PORT=4317
# Native default is ./data. The native LXC unit uses /var/lib/uptime-goblin.
# GOBLIN_DATA_DIR=/path/to/private/orbit-data
# Private CA: NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS is read before this file is loaded, so it has
# no effect here. Export it in the shell or set it in the systemd environment.
# Never disable certificate validation.
# Email is configured in Uptime Goblin over HTTPS. Its app password stays in the
# private data directory's config.json, never in public settings responses.
